BT 1 0 0 1 3 120.15 Tm 9.85 TL /F1 7.9 Tf .14902 .203922 .227451 rg (In my Selenium Java automation framework, I follow a layered and reusable approach, generally based on the ) Tj /F2 7.9 Tf (Page Object) Tj T* (Model) Tj /F1 7.9 Tf (. The framework is built using ) Tj /F2 7.9 Tf (Java, Selenium WebDriver, TestNG and Maven) Tj /F1 7.9 Tf (, with supporting utilities for configuration,) Tj T* (waits, screenshots, test data and reporting.) Tj  T*  T* (At the framework level, I have a ) Tj /F2 7.9 Tf (BaseTest) Tj /F1 7.9 Tf ( or driver-management layer responsible for browser initialization and common) Tj T* (setup/teardown. I keep page-specific locators and actions inside ) Tj /F2 7.9 Tf (Page Classes) Tj /F1 7.9 Tf (, so the test classes do not contain raw locator) Tj T* (logic. The ) Tj /F2 7.9 Tf (Test Classes) Tj /F1 7.9 Tf ( contain business-level scenarios and assertions. Reusable operations such as explicit waits,) Tj T* (screenshots, configuration reading and test-data handling are maintained in ) Tj /F2 7.9 Tf (utility classes) Tj /F1 7.9 Tf (.) Tj  T*  T* (Maven manages the project dependencies and build lifecycle, while TestNG manages annotations, assertions, test grouping,) Tj T* (data-driven execution and parallel execution where required. For CI/CD, Jenkins can trigger the Maven command, execute the) Tj T* (suite and publish the generated reports. If a test fails, I capture useful evidence such as screenshots and logs. The overall goal of) Tj T* (the framework is ) Tj /F2 7.9 Tf (reusability, maintainability, scalability and reliable execution) Tj /F1 7.9 Tf (.) Tj T* ET
